1
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:23,046
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it is a common idea in the world that everyone wants to be happy.

2
00:00:25,087 --> 00:00:30,993
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, it is easy to approach the Buddha's teaching with the wish, may this make me happy.

3
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:40,862
[SPEAKER_04]: But what people might overlook is that the desire to be happy can easily become

4
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:45,200
[SPEAKER_04]: An activity, or dependent upon activity.

5
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,942
[SPEAKER_04]: I do this, which is then, in a positive sense, going to make me happy.

6
00:00:51,302 --> 00:00:59,245
[SPEAKER_04]: But those activities, in their very nature, depend on all sorts of other things that are typically not in your control.

7
00:01:01,205 --> 00:01:09,288
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the Buddha's teaching is actually not so much about becoming happy, but about being free from suffering.

8
00:01:11,601 --> 00:01:12,383
[SPEAKER_04]: But what's the difference?

9
00:01:16,893 --> 00:01:17,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyone?

10
00:01:23,656 --> 00:01:32,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, one difference might be that if I'm looking for happiness, I will actively, constantly looking for something outside of what is present now.

11
00:01:32,784 --> 00:01:37,849
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead of, like, if I'm looking to put an end to suffering, I start with what I have, meaning I'm suffering.

12
00:01:37,869 --> 00:01:41,212
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I'm looking for happiness, because I'm suffering right now.

13
00:01:41,812 --> 00:01:43,093
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I start to try to

14
00:01:44,322 --> 00:01:46,864
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the very wish to be happy is actually born from suffering, per se.

15
00:02:05,756 --> 00:02:10,779
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if someone looks for happiness, it's because he's not happy, so he's suffering in a way.

16
00:02:11,139 --> 00:02:23,446
[SPEAKER_04]: So, rather than positively doing something in order to become happy, one can also ask the question, what is it that is presently making me unhappy, and then looking into the origin of that.

17
00:02:24,346 --> 00:02:30,430
[SPEAKER_04]: But that obviously is going to be unpleasant, because one now has to come to terms with the fact that one is not happy.

18
00:02:33,122 --> 00:02:38,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And with the fact that the things that are making you unhappy are precisely the things that you don't want to give up.

19
00:02:40,712 --> 00:02:44,656
[SPEAKER_04]: The things that make you unhappy are the things that you do not want to give up.

20
00:02:45,037 --> 00:02:45,778
[SPEAKER_04]: Ironically enough.

21
00:02:46,018 --> 00:02:46,218
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

22
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:51,765
[SPEAKER_04]: So people... so that means that people are confused about what is actually in their welfare and making them happy.

23
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:52,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

24
00:02:55,773 --> 00:03:11,519
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what are the things, then, that one would ordinarily assume make one happy, but that, according to the dhamma, are actually making one suffer?

25
00:03:12,699 --> 00:03:18,301
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I was going to give the coarse example of the triad sex, drugs, and rock and roll.

26
00:03:20,202 --> 00:03:24,184
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, those kind of coarser things that go against the precepts are...

27
00:03:25,170 --> 00:03:32,137
[SPEAKER_03]: It's kind of the first thing that one needs to kind of question, if they want to go in this direction.

28
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:41,546
[SPEAKER_04]: So, actually, observing eight precepts would be bringing about the right kind of happiness.

29
00:03:43,268 --> 00:03:43,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Immediately.

30
00:03:44,809 --> 00:03:45,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Immediately, you're saying?

31
00:03:45,830 --> 00:03:47,812
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, I'm asking a rhetorical question.

32
00:03:48,253 --> 00:03:48,933
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, oh, I see.

33
00:03:50,746 --> 00:03:58,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, if you're approaching the whole question of how can I be happy as quickly as possible, would the eight precepts be the answer?

34
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:02,270
[SPEAKER_04]: No, because otherwise everybody would be pursuing these decks.

35
00:04:02,290 --> 00:04:02,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

36
00:04:05,892 --> 00:04:11,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but then the question might arise, so why is it that those things that...

37
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,581
[SPEAKER_01]: That you think are happiness.

38
00:04:14,681 --> 00:04:16,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it that they're suffering, actually?

39
00:04:16,982 --> 00:04:22,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the difference between the right kind of happiness and the happiness that you get from those things?

40
00:04:22,865 --> 00:04:25,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you actually get some sort of happiness.

41
00:04:25,726 --> 00:04:35,792
[SPEAKER_01]: As Buddha said, it's not that there isn't any gratification in sensuality, because if that were not the case, nobody would be... if that were the case, nobody would be pursuing it.

42
00:04:36,712 --> 00:04:41,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it would be just obvious to any anyone that this is not worth it, but it's not like that.

43
00:04:41,513 --> 00:04:56,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it does give a factual sort of satisfaction, but what is the difference between that satisfaction and the happiness that you would get from the dhamma?

44
00:04:56,757 --> 00:04:59,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, as the Buddha said, nibbāna is the highest happiness.

45
00:04:59,598 --> 00:04:59,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

46
00:05:00,398 --> 00:05:02,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what was the difference between those two?

47
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:33,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first one is drawing one's attention away from the nature of one's experience or the common thread of all experience and is, yeah,

48
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:39,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Investing one in that which is dangerous or that which is subject to change.

49
00:05:39,853 --> 00:05:47,496
[SPEAKER_01]: What if you still don't see that taking taking yourself away from the nature of the experience is suffering?

50
00:05:47,637 --> 00:05:55,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a more acute reason or more acute difference in the nature of, like, in and of itself, what that happiness is?

51
00:05:57,081 --> 00:05:58,702
[SPEAKER_01]: The wrong happiness and the right happiness.

52
00:06:09,073 --> 00:06:11,254
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, the wrong happiness depends on things in the world.

53
00:06:12,994 --> 00:06:15,535
[SPEAKER_04]: Such as?

54
00:06:15,655 --> 00:06:17,315
[SPEAKER_04]: Things that your senses present, for example.

55
00:06:18,236 --> 00:06:18,856
[SPEAKER_04]: More acutely.

56
00:06:19,216 --> 00:06:20,176
[SPEAKER_04]: Content of experience.

57
00:06:20,976 --> 00:06:21,577
[SPEAKER_01]: More acutely.

58
00:06:22,657 --> 00:06:23,517
[SPEAKER_01]: No, more acutely.

59
00:06:23,897 --> 00:06:24,777
[SPEAKER_01]: More acutely still, eh?

60
00:06:24,817 --> 00:06:26,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Safety.

61
00:06:27,038 --> 00:06:34,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, more specifically... Like not being bothered?

62
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:35,020
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the wrong happiness.

63
00:06:35,521 --> 00:06:36,741
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, like...

64
00:06:38,249 --> 00:06:42,312
[SPEAKER_03]: Not being bothered just like as a circumstance.

65
00:06:42,332 --> 00:06:43,772
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like you mean ignorance, basically.

66
00:06:43,973 --> 00:06:44,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And how would you call that?

67
00:06:49,816 --> 00:06:53,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Just in the... what would be the simplest label that you could give to that?

68
00:06:57,041 --> 00:06:57,461
[SPEAKER_04]: Delusion?

69
00:06:57,941 --> 00:06:58,762
[SPEAKER_04]: Or greed?

70
00:06:59,362 --> 00:07:00,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant feeling.

71
00:07:00,763 --> 00:07:01,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

72
00:07:01,503 --> 00:07:06,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the wrong type of happiness, because you might say,

73
00:07:08,159 --> 00:07:17,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, as you said, there's a course level where you're actively pursuing stuff like in the world and that that's abandoned through the eight precepts.

74
00:07:18,291 --> 00:07:24,278
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not that someone who gives that up already got the right type of happiness because

75
00:07:24,919 --> 00:07:31,481
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, you can be keeping the eight precepts and still be engaging in concentration practices that give you the wrong type of happiness.

76
00:07:32,322 --> 00:07:35,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like things in the world, in that core sense.

77
00:07:35,803 --> 00:07:39,584
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the fact that it depends on pleasant feeling being there.

78
00:07:40,485 --> 00:07:44,326
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the main problem with that type of happiness.

79
00:07:44,406 --> 00:07:46,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's based on what you're feeling.

80
00:07:50,068 --> 00:07:52,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas the the right type of happiness

81
00:07:54,412 --> 00:07:57,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there, regardless of what you're feeling.

82
00:07:57,514 --> 00:07:59,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you wouldn't really call it happiness.

83
00:07:59,436 --> 00:08:05,240
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, in a kind of colloquial sense, you could use that expression.

84
00:08:05,781 --> 00:08:13,626
[SPEAKER_01]: But the difference is that it's not... it's about not being bothered on a different level.

85
00:08:13,927 --> 00:08:16,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, circumstantially, pleasant feeling arose.

86
00:08:17,389 --> 00:08:21,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's even when the most unpleasant feeling arises, you're still not bothered.

87
00:08:23,053 --> 00:08:37,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the suttas would say, the liberated mind, whatever feeling it feels, it feels it detached, whether it's pleasant, painful or neutral.

88
00:08:37,826 --> 00:08:41,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't say that it only feels pleasant feeling, and that's the main distinction.

89
00:08:44,077 --> 00:08:54,722
[SPEAKER_01]: An ordinary person, all they know, in order to... the only way they have to be at peace, even if they're not seeking external pleasures, is to have pleasant feeling enduring.

90
00:08:55,562 --> 00:08:58,944
[SPEAKER_01]: If painful feeling is enduring, they cannot be at ease.

91
00:09:00,164 --> 00:09:03,706
[SPEAKER_04]: And... No, on the contrary, this is when they will be seeking pleasant feeling.

92
00:09:03,866 --> 00:09:04,486
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, in any way.

93
00:09:04,626 --> 00:09:10,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if, like, they can be seeking pleasant feeling through what they think the dhamma is.

94
00:09:11,469 --> 00:09:12,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's still wrong.

95
00:09:13,214 --> 00:09:21,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's still... if it depends on circumstance, if it depends on things that are impermanent, such as feeling, it's not the dhamma then.

96
00:09:21,617 --> 00:09:30,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, more acutely, it's... the right happiness is not depending on feeling anymore.

97
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:39,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we already clarified in that discussion about the nature... the difference between feeling, sensations and emotions,

98
00:09:43,153 --> 00:09:52,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you think feelings are just sensations or something, it's quite... the problem is watered down to a great degree.

99
00:09:52,877 --> 00:10:04,861
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you understand, feeling is... like, feeling is that... that pressure, that thing, that existential drive, burden.

100
00:10:05,501 --> 00:10:09,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And you recognize that you're subject to that no matter what, and you can't get rid of it.

101
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:17,065
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize now that you need to find a way to be at ease even while that burden is there.

102
00:10:17,365 --> 00:10:21,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Like as Sariputta said, all feelings are suffering in and of themselves.

103
00:10:21,729 --> 00:10:27,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha gave this simile for the nutriment of contact or pressure.

104
00:10:27,273 --> 00:10:29,995
[SPEAKER_01]: He said it's like a cow that wherever it

105
00:10:30,615 --> 00:10:35,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It would lean on, it would be bitten by gnats and flies and stuff.

106
00:10:36,122 --> 00:10:46,254
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says in the same way, it's like we're attacked by pressure, attacked by... And then he said whoever understands that, they understand the three feelings.

107
00:10:47,055 --> 00:10:48,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that existential burden.

108
00:10:50,552 --> 00:10:51,452
[SPEAKER_01]: that you can't get rid of.

109
00:10:51,552 --> 00:10:56,034
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just a sensation that comes and you sort of do this and that and get rid of it.

110
00:10:56,234 --> 00:10:59,294
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like your entire being is enclosed within it.

111
00:10:59,995 --> 00:11:07,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the right happiness would be where, instead of like replacing it with something else, you

112
00:11:09,242 --> 00:11:09,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand.

113
00:11:10,264 --> 00:11:14,309
[SPEAKER_01]: First and foremost, understanding that you're subject to this and you don't have a say in it.

114
00:11:14,950 --> 00:11:23,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that already would, if you really understand it, that would already seriously diminish the burden because, as the Buddha said, the burden is craving.

115
00:11:23,842 --> 00:11:24,704
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the fact that...

116
00:11:27,113 --> 00:11:44,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Contrary to the fact that it's obviously not in your control, like you've been doing every single... from the day you were born and before, you've been doing everything you could to get rid of it, and yet, not once have you succeeded for any, like, prolonged period of time.

117
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:49,780
[SPEAKER_01]: In spite of that, you still... no, this time it'll work.

118
00:11:49,981 --> 00:11:50,801
[SPEAKER_01]: This time I'll get it.

119
00:11:52,103 --> 00:11:52,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And it never does.

120
00:11:53,145 --> 00:12:03,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just understanding that this is not in your control, rightly, is already diminishing the burden while the feeling is there.

121
00:12:04,639 --> 00:12:06,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing.

122
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:18,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, most of the time, for people, finding peace is more like, well, I've been eating this really bitter food, and now I just find this, you know, sweet, creamy ice cream.

123
00:12:19,145 --> 00:12:23,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the pleasant feeling that people get from, you know, their meditation and stuff.

124
00:12:23,627 --> 00:12:26,789
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just replacing the other stuff that they would have.

125
00:12:27,009 --> 00:12:29,610
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not really making them

126
00:12:30,786 --> 00:12:36,291
[SPEAKER_01]: It's imperturbable, so that even when unpleasant feeling arises, they won't be bothered.

127
00:12:36,492 --> 00:12:41,276
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, I'll be at peace only for as long as this pleasant feeling endures.

128
00:12:42,017 --> 00:12:44,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's still suffering, because you have to maintain it.

129
00:12:46,098 --> 00:12:47,399
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's the difficulty.

130
00:12:47,959 --> 00:12:54,184
[SPEAKER_04]: The ordinary conception of happiness is dependent, bound up with doing things that need to be maintained.

131
00:12:54,865 --> 00:12:57,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you said just now, it depends on the world.

132
00:12:58,047 --> 00:13:07,294
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, sure, but there's... we have to remember that the Buddha also defined the world as in a more acute manner.

133
00:13:09,575 --> 00:13:22,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... if it depends on the five aggregates, it obviously depends on the world already, like, you know, car, family, house and stuff.

134
00:13:23,004 --> 00:13:24,605
[SPEAKER_01]: That is also within the five aggregates.

135
00:13:24,645 --> 00:13:25,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is the world, really.

136
00:13:26,966 --> 00:13:29,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your happiness depends on pleasant feeling,

137
00:13:31,417 --> 00:13:36,119
[SPEAKER_01]: In and of itself, that's really, like, the root of the issue.

138
00:13:36,759 --> 00:13:41,922
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pleasant feeling is a dart, painful feeling is a deadly arrow.

139
00:13:41,942 --> 00:13:44,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant feeling should be seen as painful.

140
00:13:44,943 --> 00:13:45,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

141
00:13:45,583 --> 00:13:46,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasant feeling should be seen as a dart.

142
00:13:48,925 --> 00:13:51,006
[SPEAKER_01]: And neutral feeling as impermanent.

143
00:13:51,426 --> 00:13:58,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you should see the pleasant feeling in and of itself, not as something you find safety in.

144
00:13:59,269 --> 00:14:00,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how pleasant it is,

145
00:14:02,177 --> 00:14:13,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it comes from something that seems seemingly not external, if it's coming from your meditation, for example, you should still see it as something that you shouldn't rest on.

146
00:14:14,186 --> 00:14:22,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because to the extent that you find safety in pleasant feeling, you are, for that very reason, making yourself unsafe.

147
00:14:22,633 --> 00:14:30,579
[SPEAKER_01]: The only true type of safety or ease is when your mind has been developed to the point that

148
00:14:32,613 --> 00:14:35,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if that feeling were to go next second, it doesn't matter.

149
00:14:35,495 --> 00:14:36,295
[SPEAKER_01]: That pleasant feeling.

150
00:14:36,815 --> 00:14:40,817
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even if the unpleasant feeling were to last for two million years, it doesn't matter.

151
00:14:41,558 --> 00:14:43,279
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right type of ease.

152
00:14:43,439 --> 00:14:46,741
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, we're talking about feelings here, not sensations.

153
00:14:46,781 --> 00:14:48,662
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that existential burden.

154
00:14:49,242 --> 00:14:56,466
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize it's quite a task to develop yourself to the point where that thing which is almost automatically

155
00:14:59,056 --> 00:15:10,781
[SPEAKER_01]: The most personal, kind of deeply felt thing is not a burden anymore while it's there.

156
00:15:13,487 --> 00:15:21,749
[SPEAKER_01]: All of the time, or 99% of the time, the approach towards it would be, how do I kind of decrease the weight of this, so that it doesn't make me suffer.

157
00:15:22,209 --> 00:15:23,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's again management.

158
00:15:23,589 --> 00:15:29,890
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see the full weight of feeling, the full weight of sickness, aging and death, as we were discussing.

159
00:15:30,491 --> 00:15:31,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that that goes.

160
00:15:33,151 --> 00:15:36,452
[SPEAKER_01]: There's actually the sutta where Ānanda was...

161
00:15:37,232 --> 00:15:42,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he was bathing with the Buddha or something in the river.

162
00:15:42,978 --> 00:15:48,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he noticed that... he mentioned that the Buddhist skin was already getting wrinkly and old.

163
00:15:49,315 --> 00:15:55,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha exclaimed the verse saying like, you know, you wretched, wicked, aging, damn you or something like that.

164
00:15:56,397 --> 00:16:03,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, look at how you make this body, you know, that used to be strong and healthy, how it deteriorates.

165
00:16:03,999 --> 00:16:14,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it means like he still recognized that existential significance of that burden, this pleasure of being subject to aging, but his mind was totally unaffected by it.

166
00:16:14,661 --> 00:16:16,942
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right level of happiness, basically.

167
00:16:17,586 --> 00:16:22,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, like, a person might think, like, or write happiness, ah, well, whatever, I don't care about this.

168
00:16:23,369 --> 00:16:24,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, that's replacing.

169
00:16:25,310 --> 00:16:36,236
[SPEAKER_01]: That's more like, there was unpleasant feeling and you replace it with something else by, oh, no, this is, this is just, you know, this doesn't exist or something.

170
00:16:36,356 --> 00:16:38,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as we were saying, denying the significance of things.

171
00:16:38,877 --> 00:16:39,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

172
00:16:39,397 --> 00:16:41,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this body is just a pile of atoms or something.

173
00:16:41,918 --> 00:16:43,699
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if it ages or not.

174
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:46,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Meaning, that's really, like,

175
00:16:47,896 --> 00:16:52,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Under the hood approach of you replacing the feeling that was there, in order to find...

176
00:16:53,239 --> 00:16:53,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

177
00:16:54,039 --> 00:17:01,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that happens because the the dominant feeling is unpleasant.

178
00:17:01,562 --> 00:17:01,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:17:01,882 --> 00:17:03,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a response to it, really.

180
00:17:04,223 --> 00:17:06,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And so...

181
00:17:06,304 --> 00:17:08,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, the right happiness would come from

